'''Christchurch East''', originally called '''Christchurch City East''', is a current New Zealand parliamentary [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]]. It was first created for the {{NZ election link|1871}} and was abolished for two periods, from 1875–1905 and again from 1946–1996. It was last created for the introduction of the MMP voting system for the {{NZ election link|1996}}. The current MP is [[Reuben Davidson]], a member of the [[New Zealand Labour Party]] who was first elected in the [[2023 New Zealand general election]].

==Population centres==
[[File:Christchurch east electorate 2008.png|thumb|Christchurch East electorate boundaries for the 2008 and 2011 elections]]
{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/Christchurch East.map|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections}}
The electorate is based on the eastern part of the City of [[Christchurch]]. When the electorate was first formed through the Representation Act 1870, the western boundary of the electorate was [[Colombo Street]]. Unlike today, the eastern boundary was away from the coast; rather, the {{NZ electorate link|Avon}} electorate covered the coastal regions.<ref name="Representation Act 1870">{{cite web |title=Representation Act 1870 (33 and 34 Victoriae 1870 No 15) |url= http://www.nzlii.org/nz/legis/hist_act/ra187033a34v1870n15322/ |publisher=[[Parliamentary Counsel Office (New Zealand)|Parliamentary Counsel Office]] |access-date=9 August 2013}}</ref>

The electorate is bounded in the east by the Pacific Ocean and in the north by the [[Waimakariri River]]. Since the [[2008 New Zealand general election|2008 election]], the western and southern boundary followed Main North Road, Marshland Road, North Parade, Dudley Creek, the [[Avon River (Canterbury)|Avon River]], Keyes Road and Pages Road, before cutting through the Bromley wastewater treatment plant to Cuthberts Road. The boundary then followed Cuthberts Road, Breezes Road and Bridge Street to the Avon River, before following the Avon through the [[Avon Heathcote Estuary]] and out to the Pacific Ocean.<ref name="boundaries">{{cite web |url= http://www.elections.org.nz/mapping/ |title= Electorate Boundaries |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |access-date= 4 October 2013 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20130208035645/http://www.elections.org.nz/mapping/ |archive-date= 8 February 2013 |url-status= dead }}</ref>

The following suburbs, in alphabetical order, are at least partially located in the electorate: [[Aranui]], [[Avondale, Canterbury|Avondale]], [[Bexley, New Zealand|Bexley]], [[Bottle Lake, New Zealand|Bottle Lake]], [[Bridgend, New Zealand|Bridgend]], [[Brooklands, Canterbury|Brooklands]], [[Burwood, New Zealand|Burwood]], [[Chaneys]], [[Dallington, New Zealand|Dallington]], [[Kainga, New Zealand|Kainga]], [[Marshland, New Zealand|Marshland]], [[New Brighton, New Zealand|New Brighton]], [[North New Brighton]], [[Ouruhia]], [[Parklands, New Zealand|Parklands]], [[Queenspark]], [[Shirley, New Zealand|Shirley]], [[South New Brighton]], [[Southshore, New Zealand|Southshore]], [[Spencerville, New Zealand|Spencerville]], [[Stewarts Gully]], [[Styx, New Zealand|Styx]], [[Waimairi Beach]], and [[Wainoni, Canterbury|Wainoni]].<ref name="boundaries" />

Population loss after the quakes necessitated expansion of the electorate in the 2013/14 redistribution, with the electorate gaining [[Mairehau]] and [[Shirley, New Zealand|Shirley]] from [[Christchurch Central]], [[Bromley, New Zealand|Bromley]] from [[Port Hills (New Zealand electorate)|Port Hills]] and the remainder of Marshland from [[Waimakariri (New Zealand electorate)|Waimakariri]].<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2014 |url=http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/report_of_the_representation_commission_2014.pdf |isbn=978-0-477-10414-2 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=26 September 2014 |page=10 |format=PDF |date=4 April 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141006075102/http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/report_of_the_representation_commission_2014.pdf |archive-date=6 October 2014 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Slightly more of Bromley was added from {{NZ electorate link|Port Hills}} at the 2020 redistribution.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://vote.nz/assets/Boundary-Review/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2020.pdf |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2020 |date=17 April 2020}}</ref> In the 2025 boundary review, the electorate would gain part of the [[Linwood, New Zealand|Linwood]]-[[Phillipstown, New Zealand|Phillipstown]] suburbs from {{NZ electorate link|Christchurch Central}}. The initial proposal was to include the suburb of [[Redwood, Christchurch|Redwood]] into the electorate, though this was changed due to public consultation.<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2025 |url=https://elections.nz/assets/pagecomponent-file-files/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2025-WEB.pdf |isbn=978-0-473-75233-0 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=21 September 2025 |page=21 |date=8 August 2025 }}</ref>

==History==
Christchurch City East was first created for the {{NZ election link|1871}}{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=156}} by the Representation Act 1870, which was passed to increase the number of general electorates to 74 from the 61 that were used at the {{NZ election link|1866}}. The Representation Act 1870 also disestablished some multi-member electorates, and the three-member [[Christchurch (New Zealand electorate)|City of Christchurch electorate]] was split up, with one part of it forming the new Christchurch City East electorate.<ref name="Representation Act 1870" />

The first election was contested by [[Jerningham Wakefield]], who had previously represented [[Christchurch Country]] in the [[1st New Zealand Parliament|1st Parliament]] ({{NZ election link year|1853}}–1855), and [[Andrew Duncan (mayor)|Andrew Duncan]], who was [[Mayor of Christchurch]] in 1870. Wakefield won the election and represented the electorate until the end of the electoral term in 1875,{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=145}}<ref name="1871 election result" /> when Christchurch City East was abolished, replaced by the three-member electorate [[Christchurch (New Zealand electorate)|City of Christchurch]].{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=156}}

Christchurch East was re-created for the {{NZ election link|1905}}.{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=156}} The election was contested by [[Thomas Davey (New Zealand politician)|Thomas Davey]] (who had been a representative of the City of Christchurch electorate for the [[New Zealand Liberal Party|Liberal Party]] since {{NZ election link year|1902}}), [[William Whitehouse Collins]] (who had previously been in Parliament for the Liberal Party), [[Henry Toogood]]<ref>{{cite web |title=Toogood, Henry Featherston, (1879–1962) |url=http://www.ipenz.org.nz/heritage/bio-detail.cfm?id=60 |publisher=[[Institution of Professional Engineers New Zealand]] |access-date=29 July 2013 |archive-date=9 February 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130209172142/https://www.ipenz.org.nz/heritage/bio-detail.cfm?id=60 |url-status=dead }}</ref> (a young engineer who only recently left [[University of Canterbury|Canterbury College]] and who would become one of the founding members of the [[Institution of Professional Engineers New Zealand]]), and [[Frederick Cooke (socialist)|Frederick Cooke]]<ref name="DNZB Cooke">{{DNZB|last=McAloon|first=Jim|id=3c29|title=Frederick Riley Cooke|access-date=23 April 2017}}</ref> (a prominent member of the [[New Zealand Socialist Party|Socialist Party]]). Davey was successful.<ref>{{cite news |title=Parliamentary Candidates |url= http://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=CHP19051130.2.19&srpos=10 |access-date=29 July 2013 |work=[[The Press]] |date=30 November 1905 |volume=LXII |issue=12364 |page=5}}</ref>

The {{NZ election link|1908}} was contested by Davey (the incumbent), Charles Boxshall (who represented the [[Opposition (parliamentary)|opposition]], which at that point had not formed into a political party), [[James McCombs]] (who was an [[Independent Liberal]], i.e. he was not part of a formal party), and Frederick Cooke (who had also contested the previous election standing for the Socialist Party). Davey was re-elected, with McCombs coming second.<ref name="General election, 1908" /><ref>{{cite news |title=Christchurch East |url= http://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=CHP19081113.2.22 |access-date=30 July 2013 |work=[[The Press]] |date=13 November 1908 |volume=LXIV |issue=13272 |page=6}}</ref> The [[Second Ballot Act 1908]] provided for second or [[Two-round system|runoff]] ballots between the top two candidates where the top candidate did not get an absolute majority.<ref>{{cite web |last=McLintock |first=A. H. |title=Second Ballot System (1908–13) |url= http://www.teara.govt.nz/en/1966/government-parliamentary-elections/page-9 |publisher=An Encyclopaedia of New Zealand |access-date=1 August 2013 |year=1966}}</ref> As Davey had obtained 55.56% of the votes, a second ballot was not required in Christchurch East.<ref name="General election, 1908" />

The {{NZ election link|1911}} was contested by Davey (the incumbent), [[Henry Thacker]] (a prominent medical doctor standing as an [[Independent Liberal]]), [[Hiram Hunter]] (who stood for the [[New Zealand Labour Party (1910)|original Labour Party]]), and Frederick Cooke (who had also contested the two previous election standing for the Socialist Party). The first ballot was won by Thacker, with Davey beating Hunter by only four votes for second place. A second ballot was required, as Thacker had achieved 32.68% of the votes in the first ballot, far short of an absolute majority. The second ballot was won by Davey with a majority of over 17% of the votes.<ref name="General elections, 1911" />

Davey planned to contest the {{NZ election link|1914}}. The [[Liberal Government of New Zealand|Liberal Government]] had by now been replaced by the [[Reform Government of New Zealand|Reform Government]]. At the opening meeting of his campaign, Davey refused to commit himself to a [[motion of no confidence]] against the government, which in turn resulted in the meeting refusing to give him a vote of confidence. A week later, he withdrew his nomination.<ref>{{cite news |title=Reform and Party Politics |url= http://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=AS19141126.2.23 |access-date=2 August 2013 |work=[[Auckland Star]] |date=26 November 1914 |volume=LXIV |issue=13272 |page=4}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Christchurch East |url= http://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=CHP19141126.2.63 |access-date=2 August 2013 |work=[[The Press]] |date=26 November 1914 |volume=L |issue=15135 |page=8}}</ref>{{sfn|Wilson|1985|p=192}} This left three other candidates in the election: Henry Thacker (who had contested the previous election as an Independent Liberal, but with Davey's withdrawal contested as behalf of the Liberal Party), George Duncan Macfarlane (an auctioneer with no prior political experience who stood for the [[Reform Party (New Zealand)|Reform Party]]), and Hiram Hunter (who this time contested for the [[Social Democratic Party (New Zealand)|Social Democratic Party]], which was the successor to the original Labour Party).<ref>{{cite news |title=The Christchurch East seat |url= http://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=CHP19141126.2.32 |access-date=2 August 2013 |work=[[The Press]] |date=26 November 1914 |volume=L |issue=15135 |page=6}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Christchurch East |url= http://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=CHP19141210.2.111 |access-date=2 August 2013 |work=[[The Press]] |date=10 December 1914 |volume=L |issue=15147 |page=8}}</ref> Thacker was successful and succeeded Davey.<ref name="General Election, 1914" />

The {{NZ election link|1919}} was contested by Thacker (the incumbent, and since May of that year [[Mayor of Christchurch]]<ref name="1919 mayoral">{{cite news |url= http://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&d=NZTR19190510.2.38.4 |title=Thacker's Triumph |date=10 May 1919|issue=725 |publisher=NZ Truth |page=6 |access-date=17 March 2010}}</ref>) and Hiram Hunter (who this time contested for the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]], which had been founded in 1916).<ref>{{cite news |title=Canterbury Seats |url= http://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=EP19191210.2.62.1 |access-date=2 August 2013 |work=[[The Evening Post (New Zealand)|Evening Post]] |date=10 December 1919 |volume=XCVIII |issue=139 |page=5}}</ref> Thacker served for two terms until 1922{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=143}} and was Mayor of Christchurch until 1923.<ref name="CCC">{{cite web|url=http://www.ccc.govt.nz/thecouncil/howthecouncilworks/ourhistory/chairmenmayors.aspx |title=Chairmen and mayors |publisher=Christchurch City Council |access-date=10 February 2010 |location=[[Christchurch]] |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100522063807/http://www.ccc.govt.nz/thecouncil/howthecouncilworks/ourhistory/chairmenmayors.aspx |archive-date=22 May 2010 |df=dmy }}</ref>

Thacker was defeated in the {{NZ election link|1922}} by [[Tim Armstrong (politician)|Tim Armstrong]] of the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]].{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=93}} The third candidate was W R Devereux, a land agent who stood for the Reform Party.<ref>{{cite news |title=General Election |url= http://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=EP19220529.2.114 |access-date=2 August 2013 |work=[[The Evening Post (New Zealand)|The Evening Post]] |date=29 May 1922 |volume=CIII |issue=124 |page=8}}</ref><ref name="1922 election" />

Armstrong successfully contested the {{NZ election link year|1925}} and {{NZ election link|1928}}s against Denis Franklyn Dennehy; his challenger stood for the Liberal Party in 1925, and for its successor, the [[United Party (New Zealand)|United Party]], in 1928.<ref name="1925 election" /><ref name="1928 election" /> Armstrong was challenged by George Frederick Allen of the United Party in {{NZ election link year|1931}}, but Armstrong remained successful.<ref name="1931 election" /> Allen was active in local affairs and was the headmaster of the Sumner District High School (1908–1933).<ref>{{cite news |title=In Canterbury |url= http://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=AS19311123.2.124 |access-date=3 August 2013 |work=[[Auckland Star]] |date=23 November 1931 |volume=LXII |issue=277 |page=11}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Welcome to Sumner School |url=http://www.sumner.school.nz/index.html |publisher=Sumner School |access-date=3 August 2013 |archive-date=11 June 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130611090000/http://www.sumner.school.nz/index.html |url-status=dead }}</ref>

Armstrong was challenged in {{NZ election link year|1935}} by S W Richardson, who was the official candidate for the [[United–Reform Coalition]] in 1935.<ref name="1935 election" /> In {{NZ election link year|1938}}, Armstrong was challenged by K I Armour of the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]].<ref name="General election, 1938" /> Armstrong died in office on 8 November 1942 from heart disease.<ref name="DNZB Armstrong">{{DNZB|last=McAloon|first=Jim|id=3a20|title=Armstrong, Hubert Thomas|access-date=20 November 2011}}</ref>

Armstrong's death triggered the {{By-election link|Christchurch East|1943}}, which was held on 6 February.<ref name="Editorial before election">{{cite news |title=Split vote |url= http://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=EP19430118.2.23 |access-date=15 November 2011 |work=[[The Evening Post (New Zealand)|The Evening Post]] |date=18 January 1943 |volume=CXXXV |issue=14 |page=3}}</ref>{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=115}}  The by-election was contested by five candidates, including representatives from the Labour Party, the Labour breakaway party [[Democratic Labour Party (New Zealand)|Democratic Labour Party]] and the National Party.<ref>{{cite news |title=Five candidates |url= http://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=EP19430122.2.23 |access-date=15 November 2011 |work=[[The Evening Post (New Zealand)|The Evening Post]] |date=22 January 1943 |volume=CXXXV |issue=18 |page=3}}</ref> The election was won by the Labour candidate, [[Mabel Howard]], and started her long parliamentary career, which included her becoming the first female cabinet minister in 1947.<ref name="DNZB Howard">{{DNZB|last=McAloon |first=Jim |id=5H38 |title=Howard, Mabel Bowden 1894–1972 |access-date=15 November 2011}}</ref> Howard was confirmed later in [[1943 New Zealand general election|1943 in the general election]], where her majority increased substantially (by over 17 percentage points).<ref name="General election, 1943" />

Christchurch East was abolished in 1946{{sfn|Wilson|1985|p=261}} and re-created in {{NZ election link year|1996}} for the [[Mixed-member proportional representation|MMP]]-era. [[Larry Sutherland]], who had previously represented {{NZ electorate link|Avon}}, won the 1996 election. Sutherland retired at the {{NZ election link|1999}} and [[Lianne Dalziel]] was first elected. Dalziel had previously represented [[Christchurch Central]] ({{NZ election link year|1990}}–1996) and spent the next three years as a list MP.

The Christchurch newspaper ''The Press'' reported on 20 April 2013 that Lianne Dalziel will challenge [[Bob Parker (mayor)|Bob Parker]] for the Christchurch mayoralty.<ref>{{cite news |title=Dalziel invites Johnson to join mayoral race |work=[[The Press]] |date=20 April 2013 |location=[[Christchurch]] |page=A1<!-- Note that the online version of this article is somewhat different -->}}</ref> Dalziel maintained that she was not yet committed to standing,<ref>{{cite news |title=Johnson declines Dalziel ticket |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/the-press/news/8579021/Johnson-declines-Dalziel-ticket |access-date=22 April 2013 |work=[[The Press]] |date=22 April 2013 |location=Christchurch |page=A1}}</ref> and only formally confirmed on 19 June that she will contest the mayoralty. She confirmed that she will resign from Parliament, thus triggering a [[2013 Christchurch East by-election|by-election]] in the Christchurch East electorate.<ref name=Heavyweights>{{cite news |last=Cairns |first=Lois |title=Heavyweights fight for city |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/the-press/news/8811913/Heavyweights-fight-for-City |access-date=19 June 2013 |work=[[The Press]] |date=19 June 2013 |page=A1}}</ref> Dalziel resigned before the official results were announced.<ref>{{cite news |last=Small |first=Vernon |title=Shearer is running of out time |work=[[The Press]] |date=27 July 2013 |location=Christchurch |page=A21}}</ref>

The [[2013 Christchurch East by-election|by-election]] was since held on 30 November 2013 in the electorate. This was won by Labour's [[Poto Williams]] in a convincing victory despite the view that significant population changes since the [[2011 Christchurch earthquake]] made the allegiance to Labour less certain.<ref name="by-election win">{{cite news |last1=Conway |first1=Glenn |title=Chch by-election an easy Labour win |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/9462536/Chch-by-election-an-easy-Labour-win |access-date=21 September 2014 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=30 November 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Gilmore |first1=Aaron |author-link1=Aaron Gilmore |title=Christchurch East no safe Labour seat |url=http://www.nbr.co.nz/article/christchurch-east-no-safe-labour-seat-ck-142209 |access-date=21 September 2014 |work=[[National Business Review]] |year=2013 |archive-date=25 December 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131225023156/http://www.nbr.co.nz/article/christchurch-east-no-safe-labour-seat-ck-142209 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Williams held Christchurch East in the {{NZ election link|2014}} against National's sitting list MP [[Jo Hayes]].<ref name="Canterbury decides">{{cite news |last1=Mathewson |first1=Nicole |last2=Stylianou |first2=Georgina |last3=Fulton |first3=Tim |title=Election 2014: Canterbury decides |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/the-press/news/10524525/Election-2014-Canterbury-decides |access-date=21 September 2014 |work=[[The Press]] |date=21 September 2014}}</ref>

Since Tim Armstrong's 1922 election win, the electorate (for as long as it has existed) has been held by Labour.{{sfn|Wilson|1985|p=261}}

